# Express Backend Conventions

## Controllers

- Wrap every async handler with `asyncHandler` from `src/helpers/asyncHandler.js`
- Use response helpers from `src/helpers/response.js`:

```js
// Prefer this:
sendSuccess(res, { blog }, 'Blog created', 201)
sendError(res, 'Blog not found', 404)
sendData(res, { blogs }, blogs.length)

// NOT this — wrong payload shape for this API and bypasses helpers/messages:
res.status(201).json({ ok: true, data: blog })
```

- Do not build success/error payloads with raw `res.status().json(...)` — use `sendSuccess`, `sendError`, and `sendData` from `response.js` so `{ success, message, ... }` and status codes stay consistent. User-facing strings and HTTP status constants live in `src/constants/messages.js` — do not hardcode duplicates in controllers

## Routes

- One route file per domain in `src/routes/`, one matching controller in `src/controllers/`
- Auth-protected routes use `auth` middleware from `src/middleware/auth.js`
- New public endpoints must have a rate limiter from `src/middleware/rateLimiter.js`

## Validation

- Request body validation goes in `src/validators/` as Express middleware
- Validators run BEFORE the controller in the route chain
- Return `{ success: false, message: 'Validation failed', errors: [...] }` on failure

## Models

- File: `PascalCase.js` matching the model name (`Blog.js` exports `Blog`)
- Always include `{ timestamps: true }` in schema options
- Use `ref` for ObjectId relationships; add `required: true` on mandatory fields
- Schema-level methods (e.g., `comparePassword`) and `toJSON` transforms -- not in controllers
- Default export: `export default mongoose.model('Name', schema)`

## Security

- `helmet`, `cors`, `express-rate-limit` are configured in `server.js` -- do not duplicate
- `express-mongo-sanitize` is installed but unused -- apply it if handling user input in new features
- Never hardcode secrets or URLs -- use `process.env`
